    "summary": "Stellar early set with Big Boy Pete, Cryptical>Other One & tight jams",
    "blurb": "The show bursts open with a rare early Big Boy Pete and a powerful Cryptical Envelopment → The Other One transition that leads into Black Peter. Highlights include tight Good Lovin' drums, seamless China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider, and an energetic Uncle John's Band encore. Recording is a solid soundboard capture, though the intro audience mics are muddy and there’s a tape break near the end.",
    "review": "On March 1, 1970 at the Family Dog the Dead delivered a set that feels both historic and freshly vibrant. Kicking off with New Speedway Boogie and Casey Jones, they slipped into an early Big Boy Pete that most fans won’t hear again until later in ’70, setting a high‑energy tone. The centerpiece is the Cryptical Envelopment → The Other One sequence – a New Orleans‑style surge where Pigpen’s harp cuts through, Jerry’s guitar shines (even if it could be a hair louder), and the band rides a tight groove that collapses into a haunting Black Peter, a first for this run. Good Lovin' gets double‑drummed treatment with crisp, lively percussion, while the classic China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue flows effortlessly. The encore caps things off with a rollicking Uncle John's Band, followed by Dancin’ In The Street and a reflective It’s All Over Now Baby Blue. Across four soundboard recordings, the Hanno tape stands out as the most balanced mix – clear, well‑EQ’d, and capturing the band’s dynamics despite a muddy audience intro and a late‑show tape hiccup.",
